  "findings": [
    "Turnip issues public WaitFence in slices up to 100 ms, treating a normal timeout as pending. The ring uses the same native enum for interval expiration and idle callback refusal.",
    "Only public WaitFence gets a synchronous clock context that records the actual idle callback refusal. The existing guard and health/poison checks remain authoritative after the call.",
    "With an elapsed caller interval and healthy execution, the pending timeout passes through to Turnip. An idle/guard refusal or observed lost health returns NotReady and preserves the existing stop path. The application finish revalidates the matching operation before carrying normal expiration across its boundary.",
    "No shared ring/clock header, ABI, wait duration, deadline or resource retirement change. Internal submit-dependency waits, kernel bring-up waits and other method timeouts keep the previous policy."
  ],
